WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: I want to do another segment on the&nbsp;
recent leak of tax information about American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.440
billionaires that was leaked by ProPublica&nbsp;
recently. Because I am seeing a ton of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.360
seemingly misunderstanding the significance of the&nbsp;
story. ProPublica leaked the taxes of some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.320
wealthiest people in the United States, and they&nbsp;
came up with something they are calling the true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.320
tax rate. Now, the true tax rate is an interesting&nbsp;
metric, but it's important to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.400
that the true tax rate is not something that&nbsp;
is related to tax law. The true tax rate, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.240
called by ProPublica, relates to how much did you&nbsp;
pay in taxes relative to how much your net worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.920
went up. And that includes something we call&nbsp;
unrealized gains in the United States. This may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.120
not be something anybody cares about, but I think&nbsp;
this is a really important detail to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:54.880
The true tax rate that ProPublica came up with is&nbsp;
based on an idea that is not how we tax people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:02.480
the United States. In the U.S., we tax people from&nbsp;
an income and capital gains standpoint, unrealized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.120
gains in just about every situation. If your stock&nbsp;
portfolio doubles, but you don't sell any stock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.080
you don't pay taxes on those gains because&nbsp;
you've not realized those gains. Now, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.960
is an exception to that, and it's an important&nbsp;
exception, is property taxes on real estate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.000
in property taxes. There's something called the&nbsp;
assessed value of your property. So if you own a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
property and you buy it and it's assessed by your&nbsp;
city as being worth four hundred thousand dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.840
you pay a yearly property tax on four hundred&nbsp;
thousand dollars of value. Now, if in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:42.480
year the value of the property goes up to 500000&nbsp;
and the city comes by and reassesses your property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.920
at five hundred thousand dollars, even though&nbsp;
you didn't sell the property, in a sense, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:52.080
didn't realize the gains, you immediately pay more&nbsp;
taxes. That is an exception in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.400
But generally speaking, in the U.S., we do not&nbsp;
pay taxes on unrealized gains. We don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.240
that for stocks. We don't have that for bonds.&nbsp;
If people want that, you have to lobby for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.880
So I think that the ProPublica article was&nbsp;
very interesting, not because Warren Buffett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.720
is doing anything wrong or illegal by not paying&nbsp;
taxes on unrealized stock market gains. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.160
not Warren Buffett is doing. It's not even&nbsp;
really a loophole. It's the way the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.120
is designed. It's not even you're not really&nbsp;
circumventing the letter nor the spirit of the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.920
in the United States as if we didn't know it. We&nbsp;
have a system that favors people who earn money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.320
from assets rather than who earn money from labor.&nbsp;
When you go out and work and you get paid a salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:44.800
or an hourly wage, you are realizing those gains&nbsp;
immediately. As far as the tax code is concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.200
you took your labor, you went to work for a week,&nbsp;
you sold your labor and you got money. You realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.880
the gains on the sale of your labor and now you&nbsp;
immediately pay taxes. We. All that income taxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:05.200
on the contrary, when I when my Tesla stock right.&nbsp;
Doubled and tripled and quadrupled and quintupled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.440
right. It went crazy. I didn't pay any taxes&nbsp;
on it until I panic sold half of it stupidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.360
because I thought Elon Musk was going to&nbsp;
be absolutely destroyed for smoking pot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.240
with Joe Rogan and saying, I might take&nbsp;
the company private or whatever it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.720
So I panicked, sold half of my stock. I realized&nbsp;
the gains and then I paid taxes on it. That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.680
way things work. So the important takeaway for me&nbsp;
from the ProPublica story is if you want to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.960
that, you have to change the reality of how the&nbsp;
tax system works. And there's a misunderstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.880
about it in the sense that the true tax rate, as&nbsp;
described by ProPublica, it's not a real thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.040
and none of the billionaires are breaking the&nbsp;
law. We just have a system where you pay taxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:56.400
unrealized gains and wages. Now, there's also a&nbsp;
different take on the story. That is pretty funny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

